NAME
    login - the shell's authentication gate

SYNOPSIS
    (automatic - the shell prompts for login at boot and after each logout)

DESCRIPTION
    Before every session the shell prompts:

        login: <username>
        password: <not echoed>

    and checks the credentials against /etc/passwd. On success it becomes that
    user (uid/gid) for the session; the prompt is # for root, $ for anyone else.
    A wrong username or password prints "Login incorrect" and re-prompts.

    Type `logout` (or `exit`) to end a session: the shell restores root and
    shows the login prompt again, so a different user can log in. Each session
    starts clean (fresh environment, home directory), so nothing leaks between
    users.

    The shell itself never exits - it is task 0, the sole keyboard owner - so
    "logout" ends the session, it does not stop the machine (use shutdown/halt
    for that).

CREDENTIALS
    /etc/passwd holds one account per line:

        name:uid:gid:home:salt:hash

    where hash = SHA-256(salt || password). The salt+hash are precomputed at
    build time (scripts/mkpasswd.py); login only verifies. The DEV image ships
    two accounts (a real deployment would set its own):

        root / root    (uid 0)
        user / user    (uid 1000)

    If /etc/passwd is missing (a freshly formatted disk, say), login falls back
    to a single root session so the machine stays usable.

    There is no `passwd` or `useradd` yet - accounts are the static file above.
    Per-user home directories (home is / for now) are the next step.

SEE ALSO
    su, logout, id, sh
